diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 0f50953..95c05cf 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -5,6 +5,27 @@ delete tasks, each with a state (`Open`, `InProgress`, `Done`), a priority (`Low
 `High`), an optional due date and timestamps. Storage is an in-memory store seeded with a few
 sample tasks; all `/tasks` routes require a bearer token.
 
+## Ownership
+
+Tasks belong to whoever created them. The owner is the `sub` claim of the bearer token, taken from
+the validated token and never from the request body, and it is fixed at creation - a PUT replaces
+a task's fields but not who it belongs to.
+
+Every route is scoped to the caller:
+
+- `GET /tasks` lists only the caller's own tasks. **Breaking change:** it previously returned every
+  task in the store to any authenticated caller.
+- `GET`, `PUT` and `DELETE` on a task owned by someone else answer `404 Not Found` - the same as an
+  id that does not exist. Not `403`: a `403` would confirm the id names a real task, which is
+  exactly what a caller who does not own it should not be able to learn.
+- A token carrying no `sub` names no caller, so it is rejected with `401` at authentication.
+
+The `ownerId` is not included in task responses. A response only ever contains the caller's own
+tasks, so the field would be a constant echo of the caller's own token.
+
+The seeded sample tasks belong to `dev-user`, the subject the development token endpoint issues by
+default; any other caller starts with an empty list.
+
 ## Due dates
 
 Every task has a nullable `dueDate`, sent as an ISO-8601 instant (`"2026-09-01T17:00:00+00:00"`)
@@ -16,7 +37,7 @@ due date the task had - send the current value back to keep it.
 
 ```text
 TaskTracker.sln
-src/TaskTracker.Api/        the API: Domain (store + model), Endpoints, Contracts
+src/TaskTracker.Api/        the API: Domain (store + model), Endpoints, Contracts, Security
 tests/TaskTracker.Tests/    xUnit integration tests booting the API in-memory
 ```
 
@@ -29,4 +50,8 @@ dotnet run --project src/TaskTracker.Api
 ```
 
 The API listens on the default Kestrel ports; `GET /` is an unauthenticated health check,
-`GET /tasks` lists tasks for an authenticated caller.
+`GET /tasks` lists the authenticated caller's own tasks.
+
+In Development, with `Auth:EnableDevTokenEndpoint` switched on, `POST /dev/token` mints a token for
+`dev-user`; `POST /dev/token?subject=someone-else` mints one for a second identity, which is how to
+exercise the per-caller scoping locally.
